/**
@mainpage multobjopt

@section overview Overview

multobjopt solves bounded engineering design problems with one or more scalar
objectives, scalar restrictions, and an optional coupled validation callback.
It provides simulated annealing, a Pareto-aware genetic algorithm, Box's
complex method, projected numerical-gradient descent, and structural automatic
selection. The public API is declared in the @ref multobjopt namespace.

@section model Problem model

- Add between 1 and 25 named bounded parameters with
  multobjopt::problem::add_parameter(). A zero resolution is continuous; a
  positive resolution quantizes the parameter.
- Add minimizing or maximizing functions with
  multobjopt::problem::add_objective(). Positive weights define the scalarized
  ordering, while objective senses define Pareto dominance.
- Add restrictions with multobjopt::problem::add_restriction(). A value greater
  than or equal to zero is feasible; a negative value is a violation.
- Optionally use multobjopt::problem::set_validation() for final acceptance
  based on all objective and restriction results.

@section coupled_validation Coupled final validation

The optional overall validator is a system-level acceptance gate. It runs once
per design, after every objective and scalar restriction callback has produced
its raw value. Its first multobjopt::scalar_view contains objective values in
objective declaration order; its second view contains restriction values in
restriction declaration order. These are result values, not parameter values.

This is useful when final assembly acceptance depends on several already
computed outputs. For example, a machine may require both a payload-to-mass
ratio and a combined reserve across thermal and structural margins:

@code{.cpp}
constexpr std::size_t moving_mass_index = 0;
constexpr std::size_t payload_index = 1;
constexpr std::size_t thermal_margin_index = 0;
constexpr std::size_t structural_margin_index = 2;

problem_definition.set_validation(
    [](multobjopt::scalar_view objectives,
       multobjopt::scalar_view restrictions) {
        const auto moving_mass = objectives[moving_mass_index];
        const auto payload = objectives[payload_index];
        const auto combined_reserve =
            restrictions[thermal_margin_index] +
            restrictions[structural_margin_index];
        return payload >= moving_mass && combined_reserve >= 20.0;
    });
@endcode

Use scalar restrictions for requirements with a meaningful violation distance;
their negative margins guide an infeasible search toward feasibility. Use the
validator for a final Boolean rule whose useful distance is unavailable or
whose inputs are the complete callback result set. Returning false does not
change numerical validity, but makes an otherwise valid design infeasible.
Because the validator has no scalar distance, rejection contributes one unit
to aggregate violation. The validator may still receive a non-finite raw
callback result; returning true cannot restore that design's numerical
validity.

The supplied views are temporary and must not be retained. Keep callbacks
deterministic and free of externally visible side effects when reproducible
optimization is required. Exceptions are not translated and propagate to the
caller. See the repository `EXAMPLE.md` for a complete annotated use case.

@section running Running an optimization

Configure multobjopt::optimizer_options and pass the problem to
multobjopt::optimize(). The returned multobjopt::optimization_result contains
the scalarized best design, every feasible nondominated design retained in the
Pareto archive, the actual algorithm used, and run statistics.

A result with no accepted candidate uses
multobjopt::termination_reason::no_feasible_design. The
multobjopt::evaluated_design::valid flag distinguishes a finite but
constraint-infeasible analysis from a non-finite model result. Restriction
scales can normalize violation magnitudes measured in different engineering
units.

@code{.cpp}
multobjopt::optimizer_options options;
options.algorithm = multobjopt::optimization_algorithm::automatic;
options.max_evaluations = 10'000;

const auto result = multobjopt::optimize(problem_definition, options);
for (const auto& design : result.pareto_front) {
    // Inspect design.parameters, design.objectives, and design.restrictions.
}
@endcode

@section reporting Optional run reporting

Optimization itself performs no file or console output. Applications can
write the final Pareto archive with multobjopt::write_pareto_front() and a JSON
run summary with multobjopt::write_summary_json(). Both are explicit
post-processing calls accepting a caller-owned `std::ostream`.

Set multobjopt::optimizer_options::record_evaluation_history before optimizing
when a complete chronological trace is needed. The returned
multobjopt::optimization_result::evaluation_history then owns one entry per
completed evaluation, including repeats, infeasible candidates, validation
vetoes, and non-finite callback results. This history describes callback
evaluations rather than accepted algorithm moves or outer iterations. It is
disabled by default to avoid memory proportional to the complete run.

@code{.cpp}
multobjopt::optimizer_options options;
options.record_evaluation_history = true;
const auto result = multobjopt::optimize(problem_definition, options);

std::ofstream evaluations{"run_evaluations.csv"};
multobjopt::write_evaluation_history(
    evaluations, problem_definition, result,
    multobjopt::delimited_text_format::csv);

std::ofstream summary{"run_summary.json"};
multobjopt::write_summary_json(summary, problem_definition, result);
@endcode

CSV and TSV tables contain normalized parameters, raw callback values, and
derived validity, feasibility, violation, and scalarized-score fields. The JSON
summary uses `null` for non-finite scalar values. No report is produced unless
the corresponding writer is called.

See the repository README and the current programs under `examples/` for a
growing collection of complete buildable examples.

@section headers Public header layout

Applications can include `multobjopt/multobjopt.hpp` for the complete API.
Focused self-contained headers are also available: `config.hpp`, `types.hpp`,
`problem.hpp`, `options.hpp`, `result.hpp`, `evaluation.hpp`, `optimize.hpp`,
and `reporting.hpp`. Their file
documentation describes callback lifetimes, units, validation, feasibility,
and result ownership in detail. Generated `config.hpp` exposes semantic and Git
information as preprocessor macros; `version.hpp` mirrors those values as C++
constants, including the description, commit, branch, and dirty state.
*/
